[Music]  hi it's Michele from lab muffin beauty  science here chemistry PhD an  exfoliation fan today I'm going to be  talking about the size behind one of my  favorite exfoliant products peeling gels  how do they work is all that stuff that  comes off actually your skin if you like  this sort of video give it a thumbs up  subscribe to my channel and make sure  you click the notification bar so you  don't miss any videos also if you want  me to talk about the science behind  other beauty products leave me a comment  telling me which products you want me to  talk about let's start by talking about  what exactly peeling gels are if you've  read my free exfoliation guide you'll  know that peeling gels are a popular  type of exfoliant product in Asian  skincare there are lots of really  popular peeling gels from brands like  cure and the face shop they start off as  a watery gel and then once you massage  it and spread it over your skin they  turn into little bits  but are those little bits rolled up skin  or are they just product this is one of  the cases where ingredient analysis  doesn't really tell us enough to say for  sure which one will be so I did a little  experiment to see if the fibers were  skin or product or a combination of both  I wore a pair of surgical gloves and I  tried massaging the gel so that I didn't  get any skin contact it was actually  really interesting when you massage it  initially there's not much product  rolling up but if you add a little bit  of oil like the oil that would normally  be on your skin then the bunching up  started to happen as you can see from  the little bits produced most of the  stuff that's coming off is most likely  product and some skin oils are combining  with it to form the little clumps but if  you look more closely at the clumps that  come from the glove experiment and the  clumps that come from skin there's a  little bit of gray stuff on the fibers  with the skin and that is the dirty skin  cells if you've ever used a peeling gel  on a flaky bit of your face you'll  notice that it's really efficient and  removing dead skin but it's not quite  efficient enough to rub off as much skin  as you think it is just from seeing they  mount that bunches up if you try the  glove experiment yourself make sure that  you're using something with enough  friction for the fibers to Ashley bunch  up when you're using a peeling gel on  your skin the water absorbs into your  skin and the oil from your skin makes  the fibers bunch up plus there's  friction between your two skin surfaces  which makes the bunching happen faster  if you're doing this on a different  material and you're missing one of these  components it won't work as well I also  thought it would be interesting to see  what the bits look like under a  microscope and here they are this image  is of the peeling gel product when I  rubbed it on the glove and this one on  the right is the one with the skin and  you can see that there's little bits of  gray which is the dead skin cells  this next bit is a bit gross but I  wanted to do this for science  I also rubbed off a bit of dead skin  from around my ankle without the peeling  gel and I put it under the microscope to  see what it looked like you can see that  the bits of peeling gel that I got from  rubbing it on my skin it looks a lot  more like the peeling gel by itself then  the dead skin by itself so it's safe to  say that most of it is probably the  peeling gel product and not just your  skin coming off so what exactly ins in  these bunched up bits of peeling gel for  most products is probably cellulose and  sometimes alkylate polymer usually these  ingredients are used to thicken up  products but if you put too much in then  will tend to bunch up when it's rubbed  on the skin  this means that peeling gels are like a  very gentle physical scrub instead of  the chunky bits of scrubbing particles  you have these soft cellulose chunks a  lot of peeling gels have enzyme a  chemical exfoliants and their  ingredients as well but you can't get  that much dead skin off that quickly  using those methods even with a really  strong chemical peel it takes a few days  for the skin to slowly come off another  youtuber made a cool video about cure  aqua gel and whether it was a chemical  exfoliant she referenced me in her video  and she concluded that it was a chemical  exfoliant she tested the product with  oil on a ceramic plate and that she  tested the balled-up bits for protein I  use cure aqua gel as well in one of my  glove experiments and I did get balled  up bits unlike her and I think the  reason her conclusion is different is  because she tested the product on the  ceramic plate which didn't have enough  friction for it to punch up the protein  test she use which is very cool by the  way also doesn't really test if the  balls are all skin it shows that there's  some skin coming off which would be the  case with a physical exfoliant if you  look at the ingredients of the Cure aqua  gel it also doesn't have any ingredients  that could act as the chemical exfoliant  plus there's the fact that chemical is  folios don't act that fast and acrylate  SAR the third ingredient from the top so  based on what we know about how these  ingredients interact with skin I don't  think it could actually act as a  chemical exfoliant but this doesn't mean  that peeling gels don't work or that  there  why they're actually really really good  gentle physical exfoliants still I  personally love feeling gels some of my  favorites are from its skin which I've  reviewed in a past video about my  empties I also really like the faceshop  white jewel I've been using Kure aqua  gel as well and it's quite good but I  don't think it's really good enough to  justify the higher price that I can get  it for although maybe I'll work  differently for you so in summary  peeling gels aren't really good chemical  exfoliants they're mainly physical  exfoliants if you're after a chemical  exfoliant or if you're not sure which  exfoliants you should be using on your  skin you can check out my video on  exfoliation and you can download my free  exfoliation guide as well if you liked  this video give it a thumbs up subscribe  to my channel  I'm also an Instagram with more beauty  tips and you can check out my blog as  well see you next time for more beauty  science

